From d468c64f9025d116b13bed041bcbce05af480cc2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tim <107831991+SyntaxError-PEBKAC@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 14 Aug 2026 19:38:14 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 11/22] ducksteps: fix About dialog wordmark overlap on ESR 153

The branding aboutDialog.css was a verbatim copy of ESR 140's official
file and was never resynced, so it missed two layout changes upstream
made for 153.

On 140, browser/base/content/aboutDialog.css carried the spacing itself:

    /* padding-top creates room for the wordmark */
    padding-top: 38px;

153 removed that from the base rule and moved the responsibility to the
branding stylesheet, which now supplies both the wordmark scale and the
space it occupies:

    background-size: auto 44px;
    padding-top: 76px;

Our copy had neither, so nothing reserved vertical room and the wordmark
painted straight over "Extended Support Release" and the version line.

Resynced against 153's official file. There was nothing ducksteps
specific in it to preserve; it was byte identical to 140's official, and
the purple is upstream's own colour.

That resync also brings in upstream's
@media not ((prefers-contrast) and (prefers-color-scheme: light))
guard, so the dialog is no longer unconditionally dark. Our wordmark
hardcoded fill="#FFFFFF", which would have been invisible against a light
high contrast background, so it now uses fill="context-fill" like
upstream's. The base rule sets -moz-context-properties: fill and
fill: currentColor (both new in 153, which is why hardcoding was
necessary on 140), so the wordmark tracks the theme in both directions.
---
 .../ducksteps/content/about-wordmark.svg      |  2 +-
 .../ducksteps/content/aboutDialog.css         | 24 +++++++++++++------
 2 files changed, 18 insertions(+), 8 deletions(-)

